d this can happen for uh you know a  specific set of reasons  uh a really common thing would be you  know  you get data from one source uh like a  database an hdfs cluster um  and then you train the model but what if  uh data in the data source have changed  for some reason maybe there was  uh you know something wrong with uh the  data  over there in the beginning and someone  went in fixed it  and performed the backfill so the second  thing  has to do with what we what we know as  concept or model drift so  the data change over time and that means  that you have to do  the data distribution change basically  so you need to do retrain  uh every once in a while if you do this  manually of course it's a really big  burden  and if you couple this with uh deploying  rarely  so then you can have many features  uh piled up and a model that  doesn't have you know that good  performance  so one of the last things is that  you know what did cathy do so she took a  problem  uh so she got her input and then behind  closed doors with her team  they came up with a solution and they  then provided you know a ready product  so uh throughout  but having no feedback throughout the  development process  so miscommunic this can lead to  miscommunication  so it's really important to have  feedback with uh  and not only within the team but also  with stakeholders  throughout this process and all of these  up uh  if you pile them up together they end up  having a decreased velocity  in you know adding value to the final  product  so then what did they do  so they sat down and they came up with  a specific set of options  uh to explore to give a second version  to the solution  uh so first of all uh  they adopted things from the software  engineering  world so for example continuous  integration  um if you're working in a distributed  team it's really good to be  a coherent in terms of code style and  do some static analysis um  in that case you can you know leave  these things out and focus on the things  that matter you know  like significant code changes also you  you need to run unit tests and check for  test coverage  in an isolated environment like a docker  image and  also do some checks in your software  packages  and also sell checks for some bus strips  that may appear in your repo  so the second thing uh  we talked about we mentioned  reproducibility so  we all version uh code like with some  version control system like kit  mostly uh why not do the same with  data so  [Music]  when we're talking about you know  applications we want to have a rollback  functionality  and that means that you know  reproducibility should be an option  uh and the same goes for um  you know so this you can divide it into  models and data  uh so we can solve the model thing by  you know applying random seeds into  our into our frameworks whenever there  is some uh  random factor there but you know we  can't  we haven't solved uh the problem with  data so  you can do data versioning so kathy here  use the dvc which is data version  control but of course you can use  other types of tools like pachyderm  which do pretty much a similar job and  what you do is that you can keep track  of data sets and models so of course we  have you know  specific uh release  indeed goes hand in hand with a specific  model performance and this model  performance has  originated from a specific data set so  all of these  need to be version you know you have you  need to have end-to-end versioning  so with dvc you can keep  a signature of the data set along with  your code which would be a hash value  of your data set and then this will be  you know the link where this will  communicate  with a dvc repository with which is  pretty much uh a git  gita like uh repo so you can  pull and push the data from a remote  source  into your uh local machine so  we discussed about reproducibility but  now we also have the problem of  manual training  so every once in a while we would like  to perform  a hyper parameter tuning and this could  happen  you know on two occasions one would be  this would be a triggered event  uh so you can have either a new release  from your version control system was  push the master so you will need to if  you have changes in your models you want  to  perform uh retraining or if you have new  features  another thing could be again in this  triggered scenario  that you're monitoring your real life  performance  of your model so if you experience that  for a certain amount of time the  performance has degraded you need to  trigger you know  a new training cycle a second  occasion would be that you can have a  repeating uh something like a crown job  uh running on a scheduled basis like  every  day every week every month uh whatever  the application  uh needs and then uh this would  run a training at scale let's call it  and  here only you know hyperparameter tuning  is distinguished for  from training only in the scenario that  you do  hyper parameter tuning on the subset of  your data  because it's you know it's so enormous  that you cannot it would not  finish in an efficient amount of time  uh and then uh and dashed  here uh arrow this would be deployed  only and  only if it gets if it gives a better  uh performance so when we jump  into continuous delivery uh to the  second part  uh so kathy and her team  they used uh keep flow so  everyone everyone uses you know tools  or a lot of people have created some  tools  for their own use trying to orchestrate  these  ml workflows um of course there are  other tools that you can use  but uh since they use kubeflow we're  going to talk about this one  so what it basically does it's it's  an end-to-end pipeline orchestration so  you can have easy experimentation and  you can have some reusable components in  the pipeline which you can then take  them  and use them in to another problem and  of course you can  organize your experiments  in a hierarchical way and  all of this is done on top of  kubernetes so giving you all the  advantages  scaling up and down when needed um  and basically having a one place for  your whole  uh pipeline like you can have model  we can you can have the artifacts in  cube flow like models and metrics  and all the other stuff so  from this um kubeflow has also  a really nice another tool called catib  so catib does hyper parameter tuning  which is  a really demanding task both in terms of  time  as well as resources so  it is used to parallelize training runs  in separate pods  it records metrics and then it will give  the best model  and the really important thing here uh  is that okay apart from the ui  uh it's basically a framework agnostic  tool  so you know it doesn't really  uh it has no idea you know what's  running  underneath so all you need is uh a  docker image that will take some  hyper parameters as input like learning  rate number of layers  uh the optimizer etc and  it will just post some metrics on the  standard output  and then you can decide you know which  of this metric  which of these metrics will be minimized  uh or  maximized it will just read that  this from standard output and it will  give you uh  you know the best model um  and of course uh this  because it's done on kubernetes again it  you know it makes it really easy  to just scale up  and run let's say uh dozens of  experiments in parallel  if we're talking about random search or  grid certs and  you know achieve your results much  faster  so the last thing that we discussed is  continuous feedback  so this goes two ways the first way is  about  continuous feedback you know from uh the  developers from people  towards the application and the models  so  one thing would have to do with of  course monitoring the application  so over there you need to always check  you know that everything's running on  time  and that you're writing in the  appropriate data sources  um and that everything is function  like functioning smoothly so  you c